From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:28:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] binder: ignore a threadpool shrink instead of aborting

android.hardware.audio.service SIGABRTs on every start on this device:

  signal 6 (SIGABRT)
  Abort message: 'Binder threadpool cannot be shrunk after starting'

It is a passthrough HAL: service.cpp configures a pool of 16, then loads this
device's Android 11 vendor audio HAL in-process, which requests a smaller
pool. setThreadPoolMaxThreadCount treats any shrink after start as fatal.

The knock-on is what actually blocks boot. IDevicesFactory never registers,
so system_server blocks in startOtherServices ('AudioPolicyService not
published, waiting...') until the watchdog kills it:

  *** WATCHDOG KILLING SYSTEM PROCESS: Blocked in handler on main thread

and the device never leaves the boot animation.

maxThreads is an upper bound on binder worker threads, so keeping the larger
value costs a handful of idle threads and nothing else. Aborting the process
-- and with it the boot -- is the worse trade on hardware whose vendor blobs
we cannot change.
---
 libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp b/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp
index 4a01d8176d..d18d1c751f 100644
--- a/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp
+++ b/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp
@@ -390,8 +390,26 @@ void ProcessState::spawnPooledThread(bool isMain)
 }
 
 status_t ProcessState::setThreadPoolMaxThreadCount(size_t maxThreads) {
-    LOG_ALWAYS_FATAL_IF(mThreadPoolStarted && maxThreads < mMaxThreads,
-           "Binder threadpool cannot be shrunk after starting");
+    // ether/msm8992: ignore a shrink instead of aborting.
+    //
+    // android.hardware.audio.service is a PASSTHROUGH HAL here: it sets the pool to 16 in
+    // service.cpp and then loads this device's Android 11 vendor audio HAL in-process, which asks
+    // for a smaller pool. Upstream makes that fatal, so the service SIGABRTs on every start:
+    //
+    //   Abort message: 'Binder threadpool cannot be shrunk after starting'
+    //
+    // IDevicesFactory therefore never registers, system_server blocks in startOtherServices with
+    // "AudioPolicyService not published, waiting...", and the watchdog kills it -- the device
+    // never leaves the boot animation.
+    //
+    // Keeping a LARGER pool than requested is harmless: maxThreads is an upper bound on binder
+    // worker threads, so honouring the high-water mark only costs a few idle threads. Refusing to
+    // boot is the worse trade.
+    if (mThreadPoolStarted && maxThreads < mMaxThreads) {
+        ALOGW("Ignoring binder threadpool shrink from %zu to %zu (keeping the larger value)",
+              mMaxThreads, maxThreads);
+        return NO_ERROR;
+    }
     status_t result = NO_ERROR;
     if (ioctl(mDriverFD, BINDER_SET_MAX_THREADS, &maxThreads) != -1) {
         mMaxThreads = maxThreads;
